# Copy to .env.local (gitignored) for local dev. Issue #73's GIS consent
# flow simply doesn't render without this set — no calendar UI, no error.
#
# The OAuth client itself is a human step (Google console): create an
# *Internal* OAuth client of type **Web application** — NOT the sweeper's
# client — and add this origin plus https://hb.twinion.net to its authorized
# JavaScript origins, requesting
# https://www.googleapis.com/auth/calendar.readonly.
#
# It also needs authorized **redirect URIs** — a separate list from the
# JavaScript origins above, and both are required, because the app now has two
# ways in. Desktop uses GIS's popup, which is what the origins are for. A
# standalone or phone-sized view uses a top-level redirect
# (`src/google/redirect-flow.ts`), because GIS's popup escapes to Safari in an
# installed iOS web app and loses its opener, so it can never complete there.
# Register `https://hb.twinion.net/` and `http://localhost:5173/` — exact
# match, trailing slash included, no fragment; Google compares them byte for
# byte and anything else is a `redirect_uri_mismatch` no app-side handling can
# recover.
#
# This adds no credential and no secret. The redirect flow is
# `response_type=token` (implicit) precisely because Google requires a client
# secret at the token endpoint for Web-application clients, and a client
# secret cannot live in a browser — so it yields the same 1-hour access token
# GIS already does, with no refresh token. Blast radius unchanged: one
# per-device calendar.readonly token.
#
# The sweeper's client id cannot be reused here, despite what issue #73's
# brief says: `sweep.py` uses a *desktop-app* client (docs/sweeper.md), and
# desktop clients have no authorized JavaScript origins at all, so browser
# GIS rejects them. What is shared is the project and consent screen, not the
# client. That keeps the intent of the brief intact — this stays an
# independent per-device credential, and the sweeper's refresh token and
# Tasks/Gmail scopes are untouched — while being something a human can
# actually complete.
#
# Deployed builds read the same variable from the VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID
# Actions secret (.github/workflows/deploy-client.yml); Vite substitutes it
# at build time, so it is baked into the bundle, as an OAuth client id is
# meant to be.
VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# The authority's origin. LEAVE THIS UNSET — it is an override, not a
# setting, and every checked-in configuration omits it.
#
# The API is same-origin with the shell by decision (ADR-0006/0008:
# `connect-src 'self'`, no CORS anywhere), so the correct value is always
# the bundle's own origin, and `src/worker/core.worker.ts` reads that from
# `self.location.origin` at runtime rather than baking it in. In production
# a Worker route puts `hb.twinion.net/api/*` on the authority worker
# (`server/worker/wrangler.toml`); under `vite dev` the dev-server proxy in
# `vite.config.ts` forwards `/api` to `wrangler dev` on 127.0.0.1:8787.
#
# Set it only to point a bundle at an authority that is deliberately NOT its
# own origin — a staging Durable Object, a second workspace. Doing so is a
# cross-origin request the authority will not answer without a CORS lane
# that does not exist, so it also needs a proxy in front of it.
# VITE_API_BASE_URL=
